# AI tool caches — do NOT gitignore the ai/ standards files, those are committed
.cursor/cache/
.cursor/logs/
.claude/cache/
.claude/logs/

# CLAUDE.md is a committed one-line '@AGENTS.md' import (not a copy) — Claude Code
# inlines AGENTS.md at load time. AGENTS.md stays the single source of truth.

# .agents/skills/ IS committed — skills are source-controlled deliverables
# .agents/ tool runtime caches are not
.agents/cache/
.agents/runtime/
.agents/*.db

# Secrets — never committed under any circumstances
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
secrets/
credentials/
passwords.conf

# MCP server credentials — bearer tokens for Splunk, Salesforce (Zeus/Kronos) and anything
# else localcoder is pointed at. These files hold VALUES; the committed config holds only the
# NAME of the variable to read (`"env": {"SPLUNK_TOKEN": "env:SPLUNK_TOKEN"}`), and
# mcp_client.py REFUSES a literal that looks like a credential. This is the second line of
# defence, for the file you keep beside the config so you can source it.
.mcp-env
.mcp-env.*
mcp-secrets*
*.bearer
*bearer*token*
*_token
*.token

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd
.venv/
venv/
env/
.Python
*.egg-info/
dist/*.egg

# Node
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
*.log

# Build artifacts
# dist/*.zip are legacy build artifacts — gitignore them
# dist/*.md for ChatGPT Custom GPTs — only create if targeting Custom GPTs
dist/*.zip
dist/*.tar.gz
build/
output/
*.tmp

# OS
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db

# IDE
.vscode/settings.json
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo

# Session journal — per-developer working state, distilled into ai/SESSION.md.
# MUST stay ignored: if it is tracked, the journal makes the tree permanently
# dirty and then records itself on every turn.
ai/SESSION_JOURNAL.md
# The run log is per-MACHINE: it records what happened on this box, and committing it would
# merge several developers' clocks into one file, conflict on every push, and tell nobody
# anything. What crosses machines is the ai/ files. See tools/scaffold_log.sh.
ai/.scaffold-run-log
.session-journal-state
.correction-capture-state
.correction-capture-health

# Scaffold staleness-check cache — per-machine, regenerated on demand.
.scaffold-version-check

# Benchmark OUTPUT — generated by tools/localcoder_bench.py on first run, and specific to
# the machine that ran it. The runbook (docs/benchmarks/README.md) and the maintainer's
# reference rows (docs/benchmarks/reference/) ARE committed; these two are not.
# Shipping them would hand every fresh clone rows for hardware they do not own and a
# num_ctx they did not choose — authoritative-looking numbers nobody then re-measures.
docs/benchmarks/localcoder-benchmarks.md
docs/benchmarks/localcoder-benchmarks.jsonl
docs/benchmarks/localcoder-eval.md
docs/benchmarks/localcoder-eval.jsonl
docs/benchmarks/localcoder-eval-baseline.json

# localcoder decision log — appended on EVERY call, at the project root. It records the
# first 200 characters of each task and the context files sent, so it is per-developer
# working state, not a deliverable. Untracked it made the tree dirty the first time
# anyone ran the tool, and it was one `git add .` away from being committed.
.localcoder-log.jsonl

# THE AUDIT LOG (#94) AND ITS ROTATIONS AND BODY SIDECARS. A HARDER RULE THAN THE LINE ABOVE.
# The decision log is per-developer working state. THIS file is draft bodies verbatim, verify
# command output verbatim, and MCP tool ARGUMENTS AND RESULTS verbatim — so it can contain a
# bearer token that was passed to a server, and every row a Splunk query returned. It is NOT
# redacted by default, deliberately and for reasons written down in docs/AUDIT.md.
# The glob covers .localcoder-audit.jsonl, .1 .. .5, and the .bodies/ sidecar directory,
# because rotation invents filenames and an exact-name rule would let generation .1 through.
.localcoder-audit.jsonl*

# LaunchAgent backup written by tools/install_ollama_service.sh — per-machine.
*.scaffold-backup

# Release receipts (tools/release.sh). NOT tracked: a receipt names the commit it
# describes, so committing it would file it under a different SHA than the one it records.
# They are attached to the GitHub release instead.
.release-receipts/
